1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,315
On a mission to Mars, the nearest flight surgeon is
fourteen minutes away.

2
00:00:05,395 --> 00:00:09,770
AstraHealth turns the astronaut into the first line of
care.

3
00:00:09,770 --> 00:00:18,027
Long-duration spaceflight exposes the body to
radiation, isolation, altered gravity, and a closed
environment.

4
00:00:18,107 --> 00:00:24,550
Astronauts need to notice the subtle changes early,
and know exactly what to do next.

5
00:00:24,550 --> 00:00:26,296
This is the Flight Deck.

6
00:00:26,376 --> 00:00:32,078
A readiness score built from six physiological
domains, not a single number.

7
00:00:32,158 --> 00:00:34,893
Amara is forty-two days into transit.

8
00:00:34,973 --> 00:00:37,327
Her score is seventy-six: Watch.

9
00:00:37,407 --> 00:00:43,950
The factors are visible: sleep efficiency, sleep
duration, and heart-rate variability.

10
00:00:43,950 --> 00:00:48,583
Open the body, and the same story appears in three
dimensions.

11
00:00:48,663 --> 00:00:54,820
Skin, muscle, and skeleton, front and back, with the
affected system highlighted.

12
00:00:54,820 --> 00:00:56,665
Every score is explainable.

13
00:00:56,745 --> 00:01:04,295
A twenty-one-day personal baseline, standard-deviation
factors, and hard rules that never wait for a trend.

14
00:01:04,375 --> 00:01:10,080
When the data is insufficient, the app says so instead
of inventing reassurance.

15
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:13,748
The daily Systems Check is a two-minute ritual.

16
00:01:13,828 --> 00:01:21,243
Vitals, load, and sleep arrive automatically from
simulated habitat sensors, clearly labelled.

17
00:01:21,323 --> 00:01:25,070
The astronaut adds mood, symptoms, and context.

18
00:01:25,070 --> 00:01:35,493
Submit, and the Systems Report shows what changed,
why, and the next action: protect the sleep window,
review hydration, repeat the measurement.

19
00:01:35,573 --> 00:01:39,730
The loop closes with a countermeasure, not just a
number.

20
00:01:39,730 --> 00:01:47,164
Consistency is rewarded with mission points, rank,
streaks, and achievements, never with health readings.

21
00:01:47,244 --> 00:01:52,110
The Mission Log records the whole loop and shows the
next milestone.

22
00:01:52,110 --> 00:01:55,257
Mental health is not an afterthought.

23
00:01:55,337 --> 00:02:05,020
PHQ-9 and GAD-7 screens live beside the physical
record, with severity scoring and a self-harm
escalation path.

24
00:02:05,020 --> 00:02:09,480
On Earth, the flight surgeon opens a separate console.

25
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:18,565
She picks an astronaut, sees the same readiness and a
seventy-two-hour early-warning view, and reads the
evidence behind the risk.

26
00:02:18,645 --> 00:02:22,170
Space weather is mission context, not a diagnosis.

27
00:02:22,170 --> 00:02:27,558
The Copilot summarises the record and cites NASA
human-research guidance.

28
00:02:27,638 --> 00:02:30,479
It refuses diagnosis and prescriptions.

29
00:02:30,559 --> 00:02:37,600
When a signal crosses a threshold, it creates one
focused follow-up request for the astronaut.

30
00:02:37,600 --> 00:02:39,758
Crew sharing is consent-based.

31
00:02:39,838 --> 00:02:43,041
Health values never appear on a leaderboard.

32
00:02:43,121 --> 00:02:47,000
And every recommendation is guidance, not diagnosis.

33
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:48,081
AstraHealth.

34
00:02:48,161 --> 00:02:50,580
A calmer way to check in.

